  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A cylindrical furnace has height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>H</mi></mfenced></math> and diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>D</mi></mfenced></math> both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. It is maintained at temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>360</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>. The air gets&nbsp;heated inside the furnace at constant pressure&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and its temperature becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>360</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>. The hot air with density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math>&nbsp;rises up a vertical chimney of diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> above the furnace and exits the chimney (see&nbsp;the figure). As a result, atmospheric air of density&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>,&nbsp;pressure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></math>&nbsp;and temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> enters&nbsp;the furnace. Assume air as an ideal gas, neglect the variations in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> inside the chimney and the furnace. Also&nbsp;ignore the viscous effects.</p>\n\n<p>[Given: The acceleration due to gravity&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn></math>]</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"assets\\qt0004_6950e72d5333.png\" style=\"width: 357px; height: 652px;\" /></p>\n<br>Considering the air flow to be streamline, the steady mass flow rate of air exiting the chimney is _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>gm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>."
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "7850",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Applying Bernoulli&rsquo;s theorem between top and bottom points of furnace :</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>&#961;</mi><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Also, since&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>&#961;</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>300</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>360</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>From equation(1), we can write</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>Required mass flow rate&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>&#961;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>7850</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>gm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
